  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/196dcb953e409bd1688e77661e4a635aa92b7edb837f26bb2666592b3ff54a90.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=2-\\frac{8}{x-1}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=9$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA=-\\,{\\int }_{3}^{a}(2-\\frac{8}{x-1})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{9}(2-\\frac{8}{x-1})\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=2-\\frac{8}{x-1}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{8}{x-1} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{x-1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{x-1} & =2 \\\\ 8 & =2(x-1) \\\\ 8 & =2x-2 \\\\ 10 & =2x \\\\ 5 & =x \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=3$ and $x=9$ is $x=5.$\n\nSince $5\\in [3,9],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=5.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=5$ to $x=9.$\n\nSo, $a=5$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=-\\,{\\int }_{3}^{5}(2-\\frac{8}{x-1})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{9}(2-\\frac{8}{x-1})\\text{d}x.\n$$",
